# ESP32 USB-serial -> stable symlink /dev/esp32
#
# Confirmed 2026-09-08 on this board: it uses a CH340/CH341 USB-serial chip,
# which enumerates as 1a86:7523 (QinHeng Electronics). Verified with:
#   lsusb                            -> ID 1a86:7523 ... CH340 serial converter
#   udevadm info -a -n /dev/ttyUSB0  -> ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86" ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523"
# The MCU behind it is an ESP32-D0WD-V3. Read your own board's MAC with
#   esptool.py --port /dev/esp32 chip_id
# It is the only thing that tells two identical CH340 boards apart -- see the
# limitation below -- so it is worth writing down somewhere local.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION -- READ BEFORE PLUGGING IN TWO BOARDS.
#   This CH340 exposes NO ATTRS{serial}; the only attributes available are the
#   generic 1a86:7523 pair and product=="USB Serial". That is the same identity
#   as the Tomcat chassis's board and as every other cheap CH340 dev board, so
#   udev CANNOT tell them apart. With two such boards plugged into one host,
#   /dev/esp32 points at whichever enumerated last -- and flashing the wrong
#   robot's firmware is a silent, confusing failure.
#   If you ever need both at once, match the physical USB port instead by
#   replacing the rule below with something like:
#     SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523", \
#       KERNELS=="1-2", SYMLINK+="esp32", MODE:="0666"
#   (find the KERNELS value with:  udevadm info -a -n /dev/ttyUSB0 | grep KERNELS)
#   The trade-off is that the board then has to stay in that one port.
#
# Other common chips, for reference if the board is ever swapped:
#   CP2102      = 10c4:ea60
#   CH9102      = 1a86:55d4
#   native USB  = 303a:xxxx (Espressif)
SUBSYSTEM=="tty", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1a86", ATTRS{idProduct}=="7523", SYMLINK+="esp32", MODE:="0666"
